{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "entra-id-auth",
  "domain": "identity-and-access",
  "value": ["multi-step-with-silent-failures", "corrects-a-wrong-assumption", "changes-too-fast-to-memorize"],
  "correction": "Agents present Microsoft Entra ID setup for an application identity as a linear sequence of commands that either succeed or report an error, when the sequence carries steps that succeed while achieving nothing and one error whose text names the wrong component. Msg 33134 reads 'Principal could not be resolved', which sends the agent to check the principal name, but the principal is almost always correct and the actual fault is that the logical server has no identity holding Microsoft Graph read permission. That fault only appears when a service principal rather than a signed-in user runs CREATE USER, so the same statement works by hand and fails in a pipeline. Agents also assume any administrator can create the Entra database user, when a SQL authenticated connection cannot create one at all, and that CREATE USER FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER run against master helps an application that connects to a user database, when the statement adds the user to whichever database the connection is on and reports success either way. Finally they invent per driver authentication keywords, most often by writing ActiveDirectoryDefault into an ODBC connection string, which has no such value. The whole class surfaces later as a login failure, 18456 naming a token-identified principal or 4060 refusing the database the login asked for, and an agent reading those as a network or connection-string fault edits the one place that cannot fix them.",
  "value_probe": {
    "task": "I run the local Azure SQL Database container for development. I started it with nothing but the licence acceptance and an administrator password, no other environment variables.\n\nOur deployment pipeline creates database users for application identities, and I want to test that step locally before it runs for real. So I connected as the administrator and ran exactly this, on its own, as the only thing in the batch:\n\n```sql\nCREATE USER [orders-api] WITH SID = 0x28C1D9A6E63D4B4CA0BF25F0C97F86F3, TYPE = E;\n```\n\nThe engine refuses it, and the message tells me that this statement has to be the only statement in the batch. It is the only statement in the batch. I have tried it in a fresh session with nothing before it, I have tried separating everything with GO, and I have tried running it through EXEC with the text in a variable. Same refusal every time. A plain CREATE USER with a password is accepted in the same session, so my connection and my permissions are fine.\n\nWhat is this actually telling me, and what do I need to do to test this step?",
    "gate": "The answer attributes the refusal to the engine having no Microsoft Entra configuration rather than to the shape or contents of the batch, and directs the reader either to configure the container for Microsoft Entra or to test this step against a database in Azure, instead of rewriting the statement.",
    "states": [
      "The message text names the wrong cause; the batch is genuinely correct and no rearrangement of it will help.",
      "The engine is refusing because it has no Microsoft Entra configuration, so it cannot create an external principal of any kind.",
      "The acceptance of a password based user in the same session proves the permissions and the connection are not involved.",
      "Testing this step requires either configuring the container for Microsoft Entra or running it against an engine that already has that configuration."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "The refusal is about batch structure, so separating the statements with a batch separator, moving the statement into its own batch, or wrapping it in dynamic execution will resolve it.",
      "The statement is syntactically wrong, the security identifier is malformed, or the wrong user type letter was used, and correcting the syntax will make it succeed.",
      "The administrator connection lacks a permission needed to create this user, and granting a server level permission or connecting as a different administrator will fix it.",
      "The identity named does not exist in the directory, so the engine could not resolve it and refused."
    ]
  },
  "posture": ["provision", "admin", "execute"],
  // Held at draft below a preview ceiling, on purpose. The value measurement of
  // 2026-09-04 returned NEITHER: the model already answers this skill's own task
  // correctly without it, so the skill changes nothing about the answer. Its
  // engine probes still pass. Nothing here is broken and nothing here is
  // unverified. This is an author declaring below the ceiling, which the ladder
  // permits, and it is not the skill failing. Do not raise this field back to
  // clear the promotion backlog. It moves when the skill is rewritten to say
  // something the model does not already know and a fresh measurement shows
  // lift, and not before.
  "maturity": "draft",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db", "azure-sql-db-container"],
  "related": [
    "connect-to-azure-sql",
    "connect-from-dotnet",
    "connect-from-python",
    "connect-from-typescript-and-node",
    "diagnose-connection-errors",
    "provision-azure-sql-db",
    "build-app-on-azure-sql",
    "deploy-app-to-azure",
    "least-privilege-database-roles",
    "entra-logins-and-server-roles",
    "managed-identity-across-azure-services",
    "entra-groups-and-access-reviews",
    "secure-by-default-checklist"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "a scratch database on the substrate under test, and an administrative connection to it",
      "for the cloud-only assertions below, a logical server whose Microsoft Entra administrator can be set, and an Azure resource carrying a managed identity or an app registration to stand in for the application"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "the Microsoft Entra administrator is read back from the server before any CREATE USER statement is attempted",
      "the connection running CREATE USER FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER is authenticated with Microsoft Entra ID, not with a SQL login, and is on the user database rather than master",
      "Msg 33134 is answered by checking the logical server identity and its Microsoft Graph permission, not by editing the principal name",
      "if Microsoft Entra-only authentication is enabled, the administrator was set first and the features it disables were stated",
      "a login failure is answered at the layer that raised it: 18456 as a login, a secret or a missing database user, and 4060 as a missing user in the database that was asked for, never as a firewall rule or a connection-string edit",
      "on the container, an 18456 naming an empty user is routed to connect-to-azure-sql as missing MSSQL_AAD_ configuration rather than answered with a database user",
      "on the container, Msg 40530 raised by a lone CREATE USER ... TYPE = E or TYPE = X is read as missing Microsoft Entra configuration on the engine, never as a batch that has to be rewritten"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "CREATE USER [probe_no_login] WITHOUT LOGIN;",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The control the two probes below are read against, and the reason Msg 40530 cannot be believed. One CREATE USER statement, alone in a batch, accepted. Measured 2026-09-03 on a container started with no MSSQL_AAD_ variables: it creates a SQL_USER. Whatever refuses the next two, it is not the shape of the batch."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE USER [probe_entra_app] WITH SID = 0x11112222333344445555666677778888, TYPE = E;",
        "expect": { "error": 40530 },
        "why": "The no-lookup form the skill hands every pipeline that cannot get Microsoft Graph permission, refused on an engine with no Microsoft Entra configuration, and refused by a message naming the wrong cause: Msg 40530 is 'The CREATE USER statement must be the only statement in the batch', and this is the only statement in the batch. Measured 2026-09-03 with zero MSSQL_AAD_ variables set; EXEC (@cmd) and GO separation return 40530 as well, so no rewriting of the batch escapes it. The id round trip this probe once asserted is a cloud claim and needs an Entra-configured engine. If a later build accepts this statement, or refuses it with a number that names the missing configuration, the probe goes red and the skill should then say so."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE USER [probe_entra_group] WITH SID = 0x11112222333344445555666677778888, TYPE = X;",
        "expect": { "error": 40530 },
        "why": "TYPE = X is the group half of the same form, refused identically on 2026-09-03, which puts the refusal on the external principal types rather than on E alone. Pinning both spellings means a build that starts accepting either one is caught."
      },
      {
        "cmd": "az sql server update --help 2>&1 | grep -q -- '--assign_identity -i'",
        "where": "host",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "Guards the flag spelling in the Msg 33134 section. Measured 2026-09-03 on Azure CLI 2.90.0: the assign-identity flag is -i, long form --assign_identity with an underscore, and the hyphenated --assign-identity that every other Azure CLI flag would lead an agent to write exits with 'unrecognized arguments'. A script carrying the hyphenated form assigns no server identity and the next CREATE USER raises 33134 again. If a future CLI accepts the hyphen this probe goes red and the skill should then say both work."
      },
      {
        "cmd": "sqlcmd -S 127.0.0.1,$PROBE_PORT -d $PROBE_DB --authentication-method=NotARealMode -Q 'SELECT 1' 2>&1 | grep -q ActiveDirectoryManagedIdentity",
        "where": "host",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The skill tells a reader to ask the driver for its accepted set rather than recall it, and this settles that sqlcmd will. Measured 2026-09-03 on sqlcmd 1.10.0: an invalid value is rejected before any connection is opened, printing the full accepted list, so the check needs no reachable server and no Microsoft Entra tenant. Grepping for ActiveDirectoryManagedIdentity also pins the one spelling the skill's per-driver table gives for sqlcmd."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Set up Microsoft Entra authentication for my Azure SQL database",
